+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This review covers the Spring Boot accounts API, its H2/JPA persistence, authentication and tenant isolation, payment
+dispatch, scheduled payment execution, and the scheme, fraud, ledger and notification integrations. It is based on the
+current release candidate in this repository and its production-shaped configuration, not only on the happy-path tests.
+
+Scores use the conventional FMEA scale: Severity (S) is 1 = negligible and 10 = safety, regulatory, material financial
+or widespread customer impact; Occurrence (O) is 1 = unlikely and 10 = frequent; Detection (D) is 1 = almost certain
+to be detected before customer impact and 10 = unlikely to be detected. RPN = S × O × D. Scores are relative
+priorities, not probabilities. A risk with high severity should still be addressed even where its RPN is modest.
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems / teams | Detection / monitoring | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| FMEA-01 | All account and payment data disappears on restart | `application.yml` uses `jdbc:h2:mem`, `DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1`, and `ddl-auto: create-drop`; `data.sql` is fixture data | Accounts, balances, payments, cases and closure requests vanish or revert after a restart; customers can see incorrect balances and payment history | Ledger, support and finance records diverge; recovery may be impossible; incident and regulatory reporting burden is high | Restart/restore test, database durability check, row-count and balance reconciliation, backup-restore drill | 10 | 6 | 8 | 480 | Block production release until a durable, managed database is configured; use migrations, backups, restore tests, encryption and a tested recovery runbook. Remove production fixture initialization and `create-drop`. |
+| FMEA-02 | Payment is executed without fraud screening | `FraudScreeningClient` and its configuration exist, but `PaymentService`/`PaymentDispatcher` never call it | Fraudulent or high-risk payments can be sent; customer funds and trust may be lost | Fraud, compliance and operations teams cannot rely on the payment control; downstream scheme and ledger receive transactions that should have been held or rejected | Compare payment events with fraud decisions; metric for every payment having a screening decision; alert on missing decision; end-to-end deny/timeout tests | 10 | 6 | 10 | 600 | Make screening an explicit state-machine step before scheme submission. Fail closed on unavailable/invalid decisions, persist `ACCEPT/REFER/REJECT`, route `REFER` to review, and add mandatory integration tests and audit records. |
+| FMEA-03 | Concurrent payments exceed balance or daily limit | `PaymentService` reads balance and today’s payments, then dispatches without row locking, an atomic reservation, or a unique idempotency key | Two accepted requests can spend the same funds or exceed the daily limit; overdrafts and duplicate payments are possible | Ledger and local account balance disagree; customer support and finance need manual reversal/reconciliation | Load test with parallel requests; alert on negative balance, daily-limit breaches and duplicate customer references; database invariant checks | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | Atomically reserve funds and daily allowance in the database (pessimistic lock or optimistic version retry), enforce invariants, and require an idempotency key for retries. |
+| FMEA-04 | Scheme accepts a payment but the API reports failure or loses its record | Scheme call is external to the database transaction; timeout can occur before or after remote acceptance, while `SchemeTimeoutException` is not converted to a durable pending state | Customer retries or believes payment failed; payee may receive duplicate payments or an untracked payment | Scheme has an instruction with no reliable local owner; operations must search by reference and manually resolve it | Correlate scheme acknowledgements with local payments; alert on scheme instructions without local terminal state; contract test timeout-after-acceptance | 9 | 4 | 8 | 288 | Use an outbox/worker and idempotent scheme reference, persist `SUBMITTING` before the call, reconcile unknown outcomes, and expose a customer-safe `PENDING` state. Never rely on an HTTP response as the transaction record. |
+| FMEA-05 | Ledger posting and local account state diverge | Scheme submission succeeds, local account is debited, then ledger posting times out or returns an unhandled error; the two systems cannot commit atomically | Payment may be sent while balance is wrong, or customer sees `POSTING_FAILED` without a clear resolution; available funds may be understated or overstated | Core ledger, scheme, finance and support disagree; manual repair can create duplicate debits or credits | Daily scheme-to-ledger-to-service reconciliation by payment/scheme/ledger reference; alert on `POSTING_FAILED`, unknown postings and balance deltas | 10 | 4 | 6 | 240 | Use a durable posting state and idempotent ledger reference; reconcile and retry from a controlled worker; define compensation/manual-review procedures. Treat all 4xx/5xx responses as typed failures and retain the original outcome. |
+| FMEA-06 | Queued payments are not executed on time | The only dispatcher is a single in-process `@Scheduled` job at 06:00; no leader election, durable queue, retry policy or per-payment error isolation exists | Payments miss their execution date or remain `QUEUED` indefinitely; customers and payees incur late-payment consequences | Scheme volume and settlement expectations are missed; operations may not know which batch failed; one exception can stop the loop | Gauge of due queued payments and age of oldest item; job start/end/processed/failed metrics; alert when no successful run or due count remains | 8 | 5 | 8 | 320 | Move due work to a durable queue/worker with leases, retries and dead-letter/manual-review handling; make processing idempotent and isolate each payment; alert on scheduler and backlog health. |
+| FMEA-07 | External integration 4xx/5xx or malformed responses become generic failures | Scheme client only maps client errors and resource access errors; ledger maps only `ResourceAccessException`; response bodies are cast without schema validation | Payment requests return 500 or hang in an ambiguous state; customers retry and receive inconsistent results | Calling clients cannot distinguish retryable from permanent errors; operations lose actionable reason codes | Synthetic tests for timeout, DNS, 4xx, 5xx and malformed payloads; error-rate and latency dashboards by dependency/status | 7 | 4 | 7 | 196 | Define typed error handling for every integration, validate response schemas, cap retries with backoff, add correlation IDs, and map outcomes to documented API/payment states. |
+| FMEA-08 | Fraud, payment, or account state is not auditable/recoverable | External calls and state transitions are mixed in one request transaction; only a warning is logged for ledger timeout and notifications | Customers may be unable to prove what happened to a payment; support gives conflicting answers | Finance, fraud and incident teams lack a complete timeline and replay controls | Audit-log completeness check; trace payment ID through scheme and ledger; alert on terminal-state gaps and aged non-terminal states | 9 | 5 | 8 | 360 | Persist immutable payment events and integration attempts, with timestamps, outcome, correlation/reference IDs and actor; provide an operations query/replay/reconciliation tool. |
+| FMEA-09 | Cross-tenant or staff-only data is disclosed | Tenant and ownership checks are application logic; entity relationships have no visible foreign-key/tenant constraints, and `ownsAccount` resolves the customer by ID without also requiring the account tenant | A customer or bank staff member may see another tenant’s balances, PII, transactions, notes or risk data | Breach of bank isolation; security, privacy and regulatory incident response; downstream consumers may cache contaminated data | Automated negative authorization matrix for every endpoint and role; tenant-fuzz tests; audit logs for denied and cross-tenant queries; security review | 10 | 3 | 7 | 210 | Make tenant context mandatory in repository queries, add database foreign keys and composite tenant constraints, centralize authorization, and add property-based cross-tenant tests. |
+| FMEA-10 | Authentication or authorization is misconfigured after identity-key/claim changes | JWT verification uses a local configured public key; roles, scopes and tenant are claim-driven; key rotation/claim contract failures have no shown fallback or monitoring | Customers receive widespread 401/403 errors, or a bad claim mapping could grant excessive access | Identity service, support and every API client are affected; emergency key/config changes may cause an outage | Token-validation synthetic checks for current and rotated keys; metrics for 401/403 by issuer/claim; alert on sudden authorization-rate changes | 9 | 3 | 7 | 189 | Use issuer/JWK discovery with planned rotation (or a tested key-rollover mechanism), validate issuer/audience/tenant/roles/scope contracts, and canary security configuration changes. |
+| FMEA-11 | Local balance is stale or wrong relative to the core ledger | The service stores and mutates `Account.balance`, but there is no ledger balance read/sync or reconciliation workflow | Customers see an incorrect available balance and may be wrongly blocked or allowed to pay | Payment limits, statements, support views and finance reporting use inconsistent sources of truth | Scheduled balance reconciliation and drift alert; compare service balance, ledger balance and transaction totals; monitor rejected/negative-limit anomalies | 10 | 4 | 9 | 360 | Define the ledger as source of truth, consume a reliable balance feed or read it transactionally, and block payment decisions when freshness/reconciliation checks fail. |
+| FMEA-12 | Notification failure hides a completed payment | Notification call is best-effort and exceptions are only logged; there is no retry or durable message record | Customer does not receive a payment confirmation and may retry or contact support unnecessarily | Notification team cannot distinguish transient loss from permanent failure; duplicate/manual messages are likely | Metric and alert for notification failures; notification outbox lag and delivery status; customer-facing payment status remains authoritative | 5 | 5 | 3 | 75 | Write notifications to an outbox in the same local transaction, deliver asynchronously with retries/dead lettering, and make delivery idempotent. |
+| FMEA-13 | Due-payment job processes an incomplete batch | `QueuedPaymentJob.runDue()` loops in one transaction and does not catch errors per payment; a failure can abort the run and leave later payments untouched | Some customers’ payments are delayed while others proceed; status may not explain the delay | Operations cannot easily identify the skipped subset; retrying the whole batch risks duplicates | Batch counts versus due rows; per-payment transition metrics; alert on transaction rollback and unchanged due rows after a run | 8 | 4 | 8 | 256 | Process each payment in an isolated transaction with a lease/status claim, record failure and next retry time, and continue the batch. |
+| FMEA-14 | Closure requests remain pending or cannot be completed | The API creates and lists requests but this codebase has no approve/reject operation; processing depends on an unspecified external/manual path | Customers cannot close accounts or receive a timely decision; account lifecycle is blocked | Approvers and support lack an end-to-end workflow; closure, reporting and retention obligations can be missed | Age and volume of `PENDING` requests; SLA alerts; reconciliation between approved closure and account status | 7 | 5 | 8 | 280 | Implement a controlled approve/reject transition with approver authorization, account-state update, audit trail and idempotency, or document and monitor the owning workflow explicitly. |
+| FMEA-15 | Service is unavailable or resource-exhausted under load | List endpoints have no pagination; queries can return whole tenant/account histories; external calls are synchronous and request threads wait up to configured timeouts | Slow or failed account, case and payment requests; degraded mobile/web journeys | Calling services time out and retry, amplifying load; support operations lose access during incidents | Request latency/error/timeout metrics, DB connection/thread pool saturation, dependency latency, load and soak tests | 8 | 5 | 7 | 280 | Add pagination and maximum result sizes, rate limits, bounded connection/thread pools, resilience policies and capacity tests; monitor saturation before customer impact. |
+| FMEA-16 | Sensitive data is exposed through logs or operational tools | Payment and account data are handled broadly; logging/trace redaction and access controls are not specified, while support data includes PII and internal risk notes | PII, account details or internal fraud/hardship information may be disclosed | Privacy incident, breach notification and restricted support access; logs may be copied to lower-trust systems | Secret/PII scanning of logs, access audit, red-team checks and retention review | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Define structured, redacted logging; never log full account numbers or sensitive notes; restrict and retain logs by policy; audit operational queries and exports. |
+
+## Priority interpretation
+
+The immediate release blockers are FMEA-01 (non-durable production persistence), FMEA-02 (fraud control is not in the
+payment path), FMEA-03 (concurrent spend), FMEA-04/FMEA-05 (non-atomic external payment outcomes), and FMEA-11
+(balance-source divergence). FMEA-06, FMEA-08, FMEA-13 and FMEA-14 require operational ownership and alerting before
+launch even if their first customer impact is delayed.
+
+The existing API tests are useful coverage for authentication, tenant visibility, validation, normal payments, scheme
+rejection, a ledger timeout and queued execution. They do not establish durability, concurrent-limit correctness,
+timeout-after-acceptance recovery, fraud enforcement, production database recovery, scheduler failover, reconciliation,
+or integration 4xx/5xx behavior; those should be release-gate tests and runbook exercises.
